- entry .HmacCipher   HMAC over a hash, as a symmetric signing cipher: `SymmetricKey[HmacCipher[Sha2[256]]]`
+ entry .HmacCipher   HMAC sobre un hash, como cifrado de firma simétrico: `SymmetricKey[HmacCipher[Sha2[256]]]`

= entry .HmacCipher   HMAC over a hash, as a symmetric signing cipher: `SymmetricKey[HmacCipher[Sha2[256]]]`
-   detail
+   detail

-       `HmacCipher[algorithm]` presents HMAC as a `Cipher & Signing & Symmetric`, so that an
-       HMAC key is a `SymmetricKey` and plugs into the same key machinery as every other
-       cipher — which is what lets `Cose(payload, key)` make a `Mac0` message from it. (The
-       `hmac` extension on data is the other, output-typed route to an HMAC.) The key is 32
-       bytes (`Size = 256`), `genKey` draws it from the `Crypto` provider's random source,
-       `sign` is the provider's HMAC under the hash's `hmacName`, and `verify` recomputes it
-       and compares in constant time.
+       `HmacCipher[algorithm]` presenta HMAC como un `Cipher & Signing & Symmetric`, de modo que
+       una clave HMAC es una `SymmetricKey` y se conecta a la misma maquinaria de claves que
+       cualquier otro cifrado, que es lo que permite a `Cose(payload, key)` crear con ella un
+       mensaje `Mac0`. (La extensión `hmac` sobre datos es la otra vía, tipada por la salida,
+       hacia un HMAC.) La clave tiene 32 bytes (`Size = 256`), `genKey` la extrae de la fuente
+       aleatoria del proveedor `Crypto`, `sign` es el HMAC del proveedor bajo el `hmacName` del
+       hash, y `verify` lo vuelve a calcular y compara en tiempo constante.

